<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>$(DEVELOPMENT_LANGUAGE)</string>
	<!-- The same two languages the macOS app declares, and for the same
	     reason: the catalogs live in the RelayiumAppKit package bundle rather
	     than in this one, so without this list iOS treats the app as
	     English-only. The list is what iOS decides the app is a candidate for,
	     and it is also what UIApplication.userInterfaceLayoutDirection — and so
	     SwiftUI's \.layoutDirection — is resolved from, which is why the APP
	     bundle needs its own copy of a list the package already ships.

	     Exactly the shipped set, in both directions. A language declared here
	     with no catalog behind it tells the device Relayium speaks it and then
	     renders English; the seven frozen locales now live in
	     apps/RelayiumKit/LocalizationArchive/ and must not reappear here. Keep
	     in step with AppLanguage; LocalizationIntegrityTests asserts the two
	     agree. -->
	<key>CFBundleLocalizations</key>
	<array>
		<string>en</string>
		<string>zh-Hans</string>
	</array>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>LSApplicationCategoryType</key>
	<string>public.app-category.utilities</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- The two keys that make the app-owned Documents folder a real place the
	     user can reach. Received transfers land in Documents/Received, and
	     these publish that directory as "On My iPhone ▸ Relayium" in the Files
	     app: browsable, movable, deletable without this app. Without them the
	     files would exist inside a container nothing else can open, which for
	     a receive app is the same as not having saved them. -->
	<key>UIFileSharingEnabled</key>
	<true/>
	<key>LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- **The camera, and the one feature that earns it.**

	     `PairingScannerView` reads the QR join code the other device is already
	     showing — the same `https://relayium.com/cross-network#c=NNNNNN` link
	     `transferPairingJoinURL` builds — and fills the six-digit field with it.
	     It is an `AVCaptureMetadataOutput` restricted to `.qr`: no photo output,
	     no movie output, no sample-buffer delegate, and nothing written to disk,
	     which is what lets the sentence below say frames are neither recorded
	     nor saved.

	     Two properties of the REQUEST are what this string is honest about, and
	     both are asserted by `IOSPairingScannerTests`:

	      1. **It is asked for after an explicit tap.** The app's single
	         `AVCaptureDevice.requestAccess` sits behind `PairingScannerModel
	         .begin()`, which is reached only from the scanner sheet, which only
	         the Scan control on the Receive card presents. Nothing at launch,
	         and nothing on entering the tab, touches the camera.
	      2. **Refusing it costs the scanner and nothing else.** Denied,
	         restricted, no-camera and failed-to-start each get their own
	         sentence naming the six-digit field, and that field is rendered and
	         enabled identically either way.

	     This key was absent through `0.3.0 (5)`, and its absence was correct
	     then: the embedded WebRTC framework's `AVCapture*` symbols were an
	     upload blocker precisely because the product had no camera feature, and
	     a purpose string for a feature that does not exist is a false statement
	     rather than a fix. `docs/ios-app-store-submission.md` records both
	     honest resolutions; this is the first of them, and the declaration
	     follows the feature rather than the other way round.

	     ENGLISH FALLBACK, as below: `en.lproj/InfoPlist.strings` carries the
	     same sentence and `zh-Hans.lproj/InfoPlist.strings` the translation. -->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Relayium uses the camera only to read the pairing QR code another device is showing, so you do not have to type its six-digit join code by hand. Nothing is recorded or saved.</string>
	<!-- Local Network access is used by both Nearby discovery and direct transfer.

	     Two different operations wear the word "nearby" here, and both touch the
	     local network:

	      1. **Finding the other device is local.** iOS browses and advertises
	         exactly `_relayium._tcp` in the local Bonjour domain. It does not scan
	         addresses, inspect the Wi-Fi network, or use a multicast entitlement.
	      2. **Carrying the bytes is local too.** Once the user picks a device, every
	         realtime lane builds its peer connection with `iceTransportPolicy =
	         .all` (`RealtimeConnectionFactory.liveConnection`,
	         `LinkWorkspaceModel`), so the winning candidate pair is routinely a
	         unicast socket to the peer's address on this very subnet. iOS 14 and
	         later gate exactly that behind Local Network access, and iOS grants
	         it only to an app that has declared why it wants it.

	     Which makes the absence of this key a silent product failure rather than
	     a tidy minimalism. Retained physical runs `0af36138` and `56e78dbf`
	     recorded both halves of the shape: on iOS/iPadOS 26 the system withheld
	     the prompt altogether and the local path simply never connected, while
	     iPadOS 18 masked the omission and made the build look correct. A missing
	     purpose string is therefore not "one fewer permission"; it is a feature
	     that fails without ever asking.

	     This value is the ENGLISH FALLBACK. What a person actually reads comes
	     from `en.lproj/InfoPlist.strings` and `zh-Hans.lproj/InfoPlist.strings`
	     in this same bundle, which iOS resolves against the user's language
	     before it draws the alert; `IOSLocalNetworkPermissionTests` asserts the
	     English file and this string are the same sentence, so the fallback can
	     never drift into saying something else.

	     The sentence describes both actions that can raise the prompt: finding
	     nearby Relayium devices and sending directly to the one the user picks.

	     Still not here, and deliberately: `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription`. The
	     same embedded framework references `AVAudioSession` symbols, but
	     configuring an audio session is not requesting record permission, and
	     this product records nothing. `docs/ios-app-store-submission.md` records
	     why that observation is not conclusive either way; a purpose string
	     invented on the strength of symbol linkage would be the exact false
	     statement the camera key spent five builds refusing to make. -->
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Relayium uses your local network to find nearby Relayium devices and send files and messages directly to the device you choose.</string>
	<key>NSBonjourServices</key>
	<array>
		<string>_relayium._tcp</string>
	</array>
	<!-- Present and empty on purpose: an iOS app without a launch-screen
	     declaration is letterboxed at a legacy size rather than run at the
	     device's native resolution. Empty means the system's default, which is
	     what a single-screen app that has no launch imagery wants. -->
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict/>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
